{"conversation_id":"4b7ced60-62f7-4668-82a3-c72aececc945","status":"SUCCESS","response":"Completed-GMT: 2026-11-10 20:52:00 GMT\nCompleted-Local: 2026-11-11 03:52:00 +0700\n\nQ1 VERDICT: SUFFICIENT WITH THREE BOUNDARY-RESPECTING LOWERING REQUIREMENTS (native-image PC→K reconstruction is in `cc3` scope inside `derive.cljc`/`publish.cljc`; runtime evaluator caller-K propagation is a continuation-seat boundary item not required for `cc3` or `D1`).\nQ2 VERDICT: (ii) SEPARATE COMPANION FILE `test/yang/latte_oracle/slice9_correspondence.edn` REFERENCING VERBATIM `:version 1` `slice9_site_tables.edn`.\nQ3 VERDICT: AMEND ORDER TO `cc2 → cc3 → D1 → cc4` (`D1` before `cc4`).\n\n---\n\n## Question 1 — `cc3` Route Sufficiency\n\n`Q1 VERDICT: SUFFICIENT WITH THREE BOUNDARY-RESPECTING LOWERING REQUIREMENTS (native-image PC→K reconstruction is in cc3 scope inside derive.cljc/publish.cljc; runtime evaluator caller-K propagation is a continuation-seat boundary item not required for cc3 or D1).`\n\n### Evidence \u0026 Severity-Ordered Defect Analysis for Route (a)\n\nRoute (a) — extending [`src/cljc/yin/vm/semantic/linearize.cljc`](file:///Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yin/vm/semantic/linearize.cljc), [`src/cljc/yin/vm/semantic/derive.cljc`](file:///Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yin/vm/semantic/derive.cljc), [`src/cljc/yin/vm/linker/publish.cljc`](file:///Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yin/vm/linker/publish.cljc), and [`test/yang/latte_oracle_test.clj`](file:///Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/test/yang/latte_oracle_test.clj) without editing any evaluator (`ast_walker.cljc`, `semantic.cljc`, `debruijn/stack.cljc`, `debruijn_register_effects.cljc`) — **is sufficient** to meet the Design §2.4, §2.5, and §4.2 acceptance bar, provided `cc3` addresses three concrete defects in the current naive lowering/oracle plumbing:\n\n1. **[P1 — Per-Occurrence Origin Loss in `linearize/project-rows`] (`src/cljc/yin/vm/semantic/linearize.cljc:188-214`, `src/cljc/yin/vm/linker/publish.cljc:312`)**:\n   - **What fails if unaddressed**: [`linearize/project-rows`](file:///Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yin/vm/semantic/linearize.cljc#L200-L214) currently accepts only a single scalar `origin` (`(when-not (or (nil? origin) (vm/occurrence-origin? origin)) ...)`) and stamps that single `origin` onto every PC (`(mapv (fn [pc [_ path]] [pc origin root path]) (range) sources)`), omitting `segment-address`. Under Owner Ruling U1 ([`1795310000000:13`](file:///Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/yang-clojure/1795310000000-owner-ruling-occurrence-identity-unitarity.md#L13)) and `cc1`'s [`macro/final-call-site-metadata`](file:///Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yin/vm/macro.cljc#L1598-L1627), a post-expansion tree contains **heterogeneous per-occurrence origins**: `[:source medium batch j]` for retained source applications and `[:expansion record-address]` for macro-generated applications. Passing a single `origin` to `project-rows` would overwrite `[:expansion record-address]` origins on macro-generated `:call` instructions and fail Design §2.4/§4.2 step 5 reconstruction.\n   - **Minimal boundary-respecting remedy**: Extend `linearize/project-rows` (or add a provenance-projection arity/helper in `linearize.cljc`) to accept an optional `path -\u003e origin` lookup map (derived from the C1 side table `[origin root-address path :yang/call-site 1]`) alongside the fallback admission `origin`, and prepend `segment-address` (`(:address result)`, i.e. `(ucf/code-address code)` at [`linearize.cljc:211`](file:///Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yin/vm/semantic/linearize.cljc#L211)) so each emitted provenance row has the canonical 5-tuple shape `[segment-address pc origin root-address path]` ([`docs/design/yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md:447,920-924`](file:///Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md#L447)). In [`linearize/project-datoms`](file:///Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yin/vm/semantic/linearize.cljc#L298-L302), `flatten-tree` already computes `sources` as `[[id path] ...]` and discards `path` at line 302 (`(mapv (fn [pc [id _]] [pc id]) (range) sources)`); retain `path` or provide the entity-to-occurrence bridge with [`encoder/datom-entity-occurrences`](file:///Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yin/vm/encoder.cljc#L67-L93) as required by Design §2.4 ([`1794670000000:77`](file:///Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/yang-clojure/1794670000000-architect-callsite-generalization-design.gpt-6-astra.findings.md#L77)).\n\n2. **[P1 — Native-Image `PC → K` Reconstruction Scope \u0026 Validator Contract Guard] (`src/cljc/yin/vm/semantic/derive.cljc:60-184`, `src/cljc/yin/vm/linker.cljc:2121-2133`, `src/cljc/yin/vm/debruijn_register_code.cljc:699-707`)**:\n   - **Scope classification**: Native-image `PC → K` reconstruction (Design §2.4, [`1794670000000:75`](file:///Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/yang-clojure/1794670000000-architect-callsite-generalization-design.gpt-6-astra.findings.md#L75)) is **in `cc3` scope** because it is a pure static lowering derivation in `derive.cljc` and `publish.cljc` — it requires **zero** evaluator/runtime changes.\n   - **How it works statically**:\n     - `derive/resolve` ([`derive.cljc:21-42`](file:///Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yin/vm/semantic/derive.cljc#L21-L42)) parses the semantic vector `v` via [`code/parse`](file:///Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yin/vm/semantic/code.cljc#L349-L435) into expression trees `{:op mnemonic :pc semantic-pc :children [...]}` while preserving semantic PCs.\n     - `derive/register-image` ([`derive.cljc:154-184`](file:///Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yin/vm/semantic/derive.cljc#L154-L184)) preserves `v`'s exact instruction indices (`(mapv (fn [pc t] ...) (range (count rv)) rv)` at `:162-176`), so register `pc == semantic-pc` for every `:call` instruction, with `segment-address = (rcode/register-hash r-image)`.\n     - `derive/stack-image` ([`derive.cljc:60-120`](file:///Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yin/vm/semantic/derive.cljc#L60-L120)) walks each parsed `{:op :pc :children}` node and at `:call` (`:92-93`) emits `[:push]` for each child followed by `(emit! [:call (count y) z])`. At that `emit!`, the stack PC is `(count @image)` and the semantic PC is `(:pc node)`. `stack-image` already records `{:kind :closure :source pc}` and `{:kind :var :source pc}` in `:side-table` (`:79,88,90,120`).\n   - **What fails if done carelessly**: Over 40 call sites across `src/` and `test/` (including [`linker/relowered`](file:///Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yin/vm/linker.cljc#L2131-L2132) and [`rcode/register-image-defect`](file:///Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yin/vm/debruijn_register_code.cljc#L699-L707)) pass `(derive/register-image v)` directly to `rcode/register-hash`, `rcode/register-image-defect`, and `rvm/create-vm`.\n   - **Minimal boundary-respecting remedy**: Keep the return shapes of `(:image (derive/stack-image v))` and `(derive/register-image v)` byte-identical so existing hashes and lowering profiles (`linker/stack-lowering-profile`, `linker/register-lowering-profile`) are untouched. Record `:call` (and `:ffi-call`) `stack-pc -\u003e semantic-pc` in `stack-image`'s `:side-table` (at [`derive.cljc:92-95`](file:///Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yin/vm/semantic/derive.cljc#L92-L95)) and add a pure helper in `derive.cljc` (e.g., `derive/instruction-provenance`) that composes semantic provenance `[[sem-addr sem-pc origin root path] ...]` with stack (`h` = `(dcode/image-hash h-image)`) and register (`r` = `(rcode/register-hash r-image)`) PC mappings to emit `[segment-address native-pc origin root-address path]`. Persist the instruction-provenance relations in [`publish-closure!`](file:///Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yin/vm/linker/publish.cljc#L356-L368) beside `:yang.call-site/table` (outside the closed schema-1 manifest hash, preserving `83b52a0b`).\n\n3. **[P1 — Path Shift Under `kernel-stamp-sites` in the Oracle Bridge] (`test/yang/latte_oracle_test.clj:2192-2320, 2449-2476`)**:\n   - **What fails if unaddressed**: In [`kernel-compile`](file:///Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/test/yang/latte_oracle_test.clj#L2460-L2465), `(yang/compile-program program true opts)` produces the unstamped C1-annotated map AST `compiled`. Then [`kernel-stamp-sites`](file:///Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/test/yang/latte_oracle_test.clj#L2227-L2237) rewrites every stamped call's `args-node` into `(data/nth (dsg__intra_call gate-site! (conj [] k args-node)) 1)`. When a guest call is nested inside the arguments of an outer stamped guest call (`:2261, :2277`), the wrapper shifts the inner call's structural `path` in `stamped` relative to `compiled`, and adds 3 synthetic `:application` nodes (`data/nth`, `dsg__intra_call`, `conj`) per stamped site.\n   - **Minimal boundary-respecting remedy**: During `kernel-stamp-sites` (or a paired walk over the unstamped `compiled` AST before/during stamping), track the exact grammar structural `path` (using `vm/semantic-bytecode-grammar` slot positions, matching [`linearize/slot-position`](file:///Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yin/vm/semantic/linearize.cljc#L33-L43)) in the **unstamped** compiled tree (and, if stamping is retained for runtime execution, also in the C1-re-annotated `stamped` tree). Pass the unstamped C program through `encoder/program-batch` → `macro/expand-batch` (with explicit fixture admission origin) so every stamped integer site `k` (`1..N`) binds 1-to-1 to its canonical persisted occurrence key `K = [origin root-address path]` in the post-expansion C1 side table.\n   - **Why evaluator runtime changes are NOT needed**: The oracle's guest-level `gate-site!` cell instrumentation ([`test/yang/latte_oracle_test.clj:1983-2013`](file:///Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/test/yang/latte_oracle_test.clj#L1983-L2013)) is explicitly licensed by Signed Addendum A §A1/§A3 ([`1794630000000:13,21`](file:///Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/yang-clojure/1794630000000-architect-attribution-addendum-a-RECORD.md#L13)) and Design §2.5/§4.1 ([`1794670000000:81-101, 219`](file:///Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/yang-clojure/1794670000000-architect-callsite-generalization-design.gpt-6-astra.findings.md#L81-L101)). It already captures the caller site after operand evaluation and before callee body entry in both `:c-walker` and `:c-semantic` within the 4,000,000-instruction budget. Binding integer `k` to persisted `K = [origin root-address path]` via `:yang.correspondence/origin-bindings` (`[[site-id K] ...]`) satisfies Design §2.5 and §4.2 (independent reconstruction, exact coverage of all `:application` occurrences including uncredited helpers, and exact multiplicities) while leaving `ast_walker`, `semantic`, `stack`, and `register` evaluators untouched for the continuation-semantics seat.\n\n---\n\n## Question 2 — `D1` Evidence Format\n\n`Q2 VERDICT: (ii) SEPARATE COMPANION FILE test/yang/latte_oracle/slice9_correspondence.edn REFERENCING VERBATIM :version 1 slice9_site_tables.edn.`\n\n### Evidence-Backed Reasoning\n\n1. **Compatibility Argument (Complete Grep of Readers \u0026 Writers)**:\n   - Across `src/`, `test/`, and `docs/`, [`test/yang/latte_oracle/slice9_site_tables.edn`](file:///Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/test/yang/latte_oracle/slice9_site_tables.edn) has **only one code reader** and **one code writer**:\n     - Reader: [`test/yang/latte_oracle_test.clj:3412-3415`](file:///Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/test/yang/latte_oracle_test.clj#L3412-L3415) (`test-kernel-gate-evidence-writer-round-trip`), which asserts `(= 1 (:version tables))` and `(= (count (:fixtures written)) (count (:fixtures tables)))`.\n     - Writer: [`test/yang/latte_oracle_test.clj:3284-3293`](file:///Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/test/yang/latte_oracle_test.clj#L3284-L3293) (`write-kernel-gate-evidence!`, plus temp-file cleanup at `:3418`).\n     - Metadata pointer: [`test/yang/latte_oracle_test.clj:3247`](file:///Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/test/yang/latte_oracle_test.clj#L3247) and [`test/yang/latte_oracle/slice9_evidence.edn:25`](file:///Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/test/yang/latte_oracle/slice9_evidence.edn#L25) (`:site-tables-file \"slice9_site_tables.edn\"`).\n     - Prose citations: [`test/yang/latte_oracle/slice9_gate.md:24,228`](file:///Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/test/yang/latte_oracle/slice9_gate.md#L24) and [`test/yang/latte_oracle/attribution_inventory.md:202`](file:///Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/test/yang/latte_oracle/attribution_inventory.md#L202).\n   - However, **in-memory consumers** of `(:site-table row)` in `test/yang/latte_oracle_test.clj` require homogeneous keys matching the third element of `:executed-calls` (`[fname arity site-id]` in `slice9_evidence.edn`):\n     - [`kernel-stale-sites`](file:///Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/test/yang/latte_oracle_test.clj#L2368-L2382) iterates `(for [[k s] table :when (and (symbol? (:callee s)) (not (contains? recorded k)))] k)` and calls `(sort ...)` at line 2380.\n     - Under choice (i), dual-keying `:site-table` in-place with both integer keys (`1..N`) and vector keys (`K = [origin root-address path]`) while `:executed-calls` in `slice9_evidence.edn` records integer `1..N` causes **two immediate breakages**: (a) every `K` key is not in `recorded` and is falsely reported as a stale site, and (b) `(sort ...)` over mixed `java.lang.Long` and `PersistentVector` keys throws `ClassCastException`!\n\n2. **Failure-Mode \u0026 Contract Argument (Design §1, §2.5, §3.4, §4.1, §4.2 and Unitarity)**:\n   - **Design §1 (`1794670000000:12`)**: *\"Publish the durable correspondence as an additional addressable record referring to the oracle's evidence **without changing that evidence's format**.\"*\n   - **Design §2.5 (`1794670000000:97`)**: *\"preserve its evidence verbatim and publish a reviewed binding of its origin fields to this semantic domain; **do not rewrite its tables behind an unchanged version**.\"*\n   - **Design §4.1 (`1794670000000:183-220`)**: Defines `:yang.correspondence/schema 1` with a **closed key set** (`:200`), where `:yang.correspondence/evidence` points to `\u003cJing address of OracleEvidence\u003e` (\"**OracleEvidence verbatim**: same EDN data shape, schema/version, field names, vectors, site ids, source-hash value and classifications. Do not project a subset or copy its rows into a second durable site-table shape\", `:202`), and `:yang.correspondence/origin-bindings` (`[[\u003cephemeral site id from OracleEvidence\u003e \u003cpersisted occurrence key\u003e] ...]`, `:188-189, 219`) binds the oracle's integer site IDs to persisted C1 occurrence keys `K = [origin root-address path]`.\n   - **Comment in `test/yang/latte_oracle_test.clj:3281-3283`**: *\"one derived table and one canonical-source-hash per fixture, stable across refreshes so the later durable correspondence record lands additively\"* — keeping `slice9_site_tables.edn` byte-stable while landing `slice9_correspondence.edn` beside it honors this comment literally and matches the `83b52a0b` (`:yang.call-site/schema 1` naming `:yang.call-site/manifest`) and `yang.python.antlr.siteset` (`siteset.cljc:5-22`) precedents cited in Design §4.2 (`:223`).\n   - **Failure-mode separation (Design §3.4, `:171-174`)**: With a separate `slice9_correspondence.edn`, any corruption, recipe mismatch, or origin-binding failure in `D1` fails D1 correspondence verification cleanly without mutating or invalidating the accepted `:version 1` oracle evidence (`slice9_site_tables.edn` and `slice9_evidence.edn`), and never rewrites historical evidence.\n\n---\n\n## Question 3 — Decomposition Confirmation\n\n`Q3 VERDICT: AMEND ORDER TO cc2 → cc3 → D1 → cc4 (D1 before cc4).`\n\n### Dependency Analysis \u0026 Ordering Justification\n\n1. **Does `D1` depend on `cc4` (four-host equality + budget gate)? No.**\n   - `D1` produces `test/yang/latte_oracle/slice9_correspondence.edn`, the `:yang.correspondence.recipe/schema 1` recipe, and the 7-step independent reconstruction verifier ([`Design §4.2:225-235`](file:///Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/yang-clojure/1794670000000-architect-callsite-generalization-design.gpt-6-astra.findings.md#L225-L235)) in `test/yang/latte_oracle_test.clj` / `src/dev/latte_oracle_tables.clj`.\n   - The LaTTe oracle is JVM-only (`.clj`). Its prerequisites are `cc1` (landed), `cc2` (in flight on `cc2-transform-session-c1`), and `cc3` (lowering provenance in `linearize.cljc`/`derive.cljc`/`publish.cljc` + the oracle `site-id ↔ K` binding). Once `cc3` lands, `D1` has zero dependency on `cc4`'s Node, Dart, or Jolt host lanes.\n\n2. **Does `cc4` depend on `D1`? Yes — by both the signed acceptance sequence and the budget specification:**\n   - **Design §8 Steps 3 → 4 → 5 (`1794670000000:373-375`) and Signed Addendum A §A4 (`1794630000000:25`)**:\n     - Step 3 (`cc1`–`cc3`) implements common occurrence/side-table production, preservation, and publication.\n     - Step 4 (`D1`) implements and independently reconstructs `D1` **before** Step 5 (`cc4`, accepting each production frontend after its four-host brief, cross-host equality, and budget measurements pass).\n     - Signed Addendum A §A4 (`1794630000000:25`) explicitly strikes *\"or atomically with\"*: D1 implementation and independent verification must complete before accepting reliance.\n   - **Design §5.3 (`1794670000000:284`) \u0026 §6.1 (`1794670000000:296`)**:\n     - Design §6.1 defines the mandatory static budget `S_static` (measured in `cc4`) to include *\"exact encoded bytes of C1 rows, provenance tables, retained origin records, **D1 and its reconstruction inputs**\"*, and Design §5.3 requires the frontend acceptance bundle (`cc4`) to include the completed D1 verification result. Running `D1` before `cc4` allows `cc4` to measure the final `D1` artifact bytes in `S_static` and include the D1 verification result in a single pass.\n   - **Engineering Risk Reduction**:\n     - Running `D1`'s 7-step independent reconstruction verifier immediately after `cc3` stress-tests `cc3`'s C1 side-table and instruction-provenance schemas on the full 54-fixture LaTTe corpus *before* running the expensive four-host (JVM, Node, Dart, Jolt) replay equality and prelude republication battery in `cc4`. If `D1` exposes any schema or provenance defect in `cc3`, fixing it before `cc4` avoids repeating the 4-host verification run.\n\n---\n\n## Risks Surfaced to the Owner\n\n1. **Exception-Track (B)-Fallback Pre-Claim Hazard (`collab/exception-track/1794343000000-exception-track-surface-registry.md:32-36, 54-58`)**:\n   - The continuation-semantics seat has noted a conditional `(B)` fallback if Clojure R4 equivalence proof fails, which could touch `clojure.cljc` broadly or pre-claim `encoder.cljc`, `publish.cljc`, `linearize.cljc`, or `derive.cljc`. Because `cc3` touches `linearize.cljc`, `derive.cljc`, and `publish.cljc`, dispatching `cc3` promptly after `cc2` lands (while Clojure R3/R4 is still in its `clojure.cljc`-internal phase) avoids any file-lock contention.\n2. **Untracked vs. Tracked Code Size of `slice9_correspondence.edn` (`Design §6.1, §6.3`)**:\n   - In `D1`, storing full C1 metadata rows and instruction-provenance tables inline for all 54 LaTTe fixtures inside a single checked-in EDN file could make `slice9_correspondence.edn` very large (tens of megabytes) because each fixture compiles the inlined LaTTe kernel + preludeclosure. To keep `slice9_correspondence.edn` compact and diffable while satisfying Design §4.1 (`:183-205`), `slice9_correspondence.edn` should store the **content addresses (`dao.jing` digests)** for `:yang.correspondence/evidence`, `:yang.correspondence/recipe`, `:yang.correspondence/metadata`, and `:yang.correspondence/instruction-provenance` alongside the compact `:yang.correspondence/origin-bindings` (`[[site-int K] ...]`) per fixture, and the 7-step verifier (`Design §4.2`) should reconstruct and verify those content-addressed relations in a content-addressed store/handle during verification rather than checking in multi-megabyte raw instruction vectors per fixture.\n3. **Jolt J2 Load Check in `cc4` (`collab/jolt/1794900000000-orchestrator-handoff-jolt-seat.md:33-34`)**:\n   - When `cc4` runs the four-host gate (JVM, Node, Dart, Jolt), ensure `yang.clojure-callsite-test` is wired into the Jolt lane alongside the pending J2 load check of `yang.clojure` so both obligations close in one Jolt run.\n","duration_seconds":313.156519,"num_turns":1,"usage":{"input_tokens":165522,"output_tokens":21613,"thinking_tokens":12893,"cache_read_tokens":1086833,"total_tokens":187135}}
